draw(P0--Q2,red+linewidth(1.1));
dot(P0);dot(Q2);
label("$P$",P0,SW);label("$Q$",Q2,NE);
label("$a$",(P0+P1)/2,S);label("$b$",(P1+Q1)/2,SE);label("$c$",(Q1+Q2)/2,E);
label("$e$",P0+0.6*(Q1-P0),S,blue);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

No two vertices are farther apart: two vertices differ in $0$, $1$, $2$ or $3$ of the coordinates (in a coordinate system along the edges), giving distances $0$, an edge, a face diagonal or the space diagonal, and the space diagonal is the largest. For a cube of edge $a$ the space diagonal is $\sqrt{3a^2}=a\sqrt3$.

question:
For an integer $n\ge2$, find formulas for
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] the number of $n$-digit positive integers whose digits are strictly increasing from left to right;
\item[(b)] the number of $n$-digit positive integers whose digits are strictly decreasing from left to right.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Let the overlap be $ABCD$, with $AB$ along an edge of the first strip and $AD$ along an edge of the second. The distance between the parallel sides $AB$ and $DC$ is the width $2$ of the first strip, and the angle at $A$ is $30^\circ$. Side $AD$ crosses the first strip, so its length satisfies $AD\sin30^\circ=2$, giving $AD=4$. In the same way, $AB$ crosses the second strip: $AB\sin30^\circ=2$ and $AB=4$. (So the overlap is a rhombus.)

